description:
LOCATION: crate-jmap-contacts-server/src/backend.rs:100-110

CURRENT DOC:
    /// Check whether an AddressBook has any ContactCards in it.
    ///
    /// Called by `AddressBook/set` destroy processing when
    /// `onDestroyRemoveContents` is false (the default). If this
    /// returns `true`, the destroy is rejected with
    /// `addressBookHasContents`.
    fn address_book_has_contents(...) -> impl Future<Output = bool> + Send;

PROBLEM:
The doc describes the trait method from the HANDLER's perspective
("Called by ... destroy processing", "If this returns true, the
destroy is rejected ..."), not from the BACKEND IMPLEMENTOR's
perspective. Trait method docs should state the contract the backend
implementor must satisfy. The current shape mixes the two audiences.

Why this matters:
- A backend implementor reads "If this returns true, the destroy is
  rejected with addressBookHasContents" and thinks they have some
  influence over the response code. They don't — they just return
  bool, the handler decides everything else.
- An implementor reading the doc gets no guidance on what
  "has any ContactCards in it" actually means: any card in any
  account? cards in this specific account? cards owned by this
  caller? cards visible to this caller? The doc skips all of that.
- "addressBookHasContents" is a wire-format error name, not a contract
  point the backend can influence. Putting it in the trait doc is
  layer-violation.

RECOMMENDED:
Restate as a pure backend contract:

    /// Return `true` if `address_book_id` (in `account_id`) currently
    /// references one or more ContactCards.
    ///
    /// "References" is defined by `ContactCard.addressBookIds[id] =
    /// true` per RFC 9610 §3 (a JMAP addition over RFC 9553). Cards
    /// referencing this address book in addition to others still
    /// count.
    ///
    /// Called by `AddressBook/set` destroy processing; see the handler
    /// docs for the full RFC 9610 §2.3 destroy semantics. Backends
    /// SHOULD NOT inspect `caller`'s permissions here — visibility
    /// checks happen in `destroy_object`.

P3 because the doc is not WRONG (the handler does call it, the
handler does emit addressBookHasContents on true); it just describes
the wrong layer. Pairs with JMAP-qz9v.27 (the underlying bool-vs-
Result signature problem).
